description:
## Goal and why

Verify on real browsers (Chrome, Safari, iOS Safari, Firefox; plus the Telegram in-app webview for the WebAuthn item) every browser behaviour the serving model depends on, **in the first week, before T16 starts**. The whole security model of partifactory rests on serving each part document with CSP `sandbox` (no `allow-same-origin`) so it runs in an **opaque origin** with no cookies, on its own host `<name>-part.<domain>`, framed by a trusted wrapper at the part host's root that gets its capability from a `#pf=` fragment. If any of those behaviours differs on Safari, the content listener (T16), prelude (T62) and wrapper (T29) must change; finding that late would mean a redesign. Items 10, 12 and 13 "decide the wrapper flow" (§5).

T72 is P0 and feeds only T16, but T16 is on the Slice 1 critical path, so T72 effectively sits on it too.

## Scope

A throwaway test server (axum, or a static server + tiny handlers) on a TLS test domain with a wildcard cert, serving: a workspace host `parts.<test-domain>` that sets a `SameSite=Lax` `__Host-` test cookie, part hosts `a-part.` and `b-part.`, a sibling "canary service" host, and a blob host. Every response uses the canonical part CSP (copied verbatim):

```
Content-Security-Policy:
  sandbox allow-scripts allow-forms allow-popups allow-popups-to-escape-sandbox allow-modals
          allow-downloads allow-top-navigation-by-user-activation;
  default-src 'none';
  script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' 'unsafe-eval' blob: <blob>;
  style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' <blob>;
  img-src 'self' data: blob: <blob>;
  media-src 'self' data: blob: <blob>;
  font-src 'self' data: <blob>;
  connect-src 'self' <blob>;
  worker-src 'self' blob:;
  frame-src 'self';
  form-action 'self';
  base-uri 'none';
  object-src 'none';
  frame-ancestors <self-host> <workspace> <embedders>
Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow
X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
```

The 13 spike items (§5), each one row per browser in the results table:
1. `'self'` in `script-src`/`connect-src` still matches the part host from a sandboxed document (fallback: list the host explicitly);
2. `Origin` (`null`) and `Sec-Fetch-Site` (`cross-site`) values on a fetch to the part's own host;
3. `fetch("./x.json")`, `<script type="module">` and `import()` with `ACAO: null` and no credentials;
4. `Object.defineProperty(window, "localStorage", …)` before any page script runs, visible to later inline scripts;
5. `new Worker(blobURL)` and the wrapper around a relative URL (WebKit has open bugs here);
6. popups with `allow-popups-to-escape-sandbox`, clipboard, `navigator.share`, fullscreen with user activation;
7. the Cookie Store API (`cookieStore.set`) and `document.cookie` writes both fail;
8. a part's fetch/POST to a sibling `<domain>` service, and to the workspace, carries no `SameSite=Lax` cookies;
9. CSP `sandbox` is honoured from the header (not `Report-Only`, not `<meta>`);
10. the wrapper flow: a `#pf=` fragment in a 302 `Location` survives to the part host; `history.replaceState` clears it before any other script; an explicit part-host `frame-ancestors` admits the same-host wrapper; and a credentialed same-site `fetch` from the part host to the workspace carries the `Lax` session cookie (Safari's tracking prevention included);
11. *Cloudflare adapter only:* the wildcard CNAME to `<tunnel>.cfargotunnel.com` serves a new `*-part` name;
12. a WebSocket from a sandboxed document to its own host (`Origin: null` in the handshake) and SSE under `/c/<cap>/api/`, both through each example proxy;
13. a WebAuthn assertion from the wrapper origin with `rp.id` = the apex, on Safari and the Telegram webview.

Subtasks (this parent is the integration/closure bead; close it when `docs/spikes/sandbox.md` is complete and every subtask is closed):
- **T72.a** - test rig + items 1-9 (sandbox semantics in the opaque origin).
- **T72.b** - items 10 and 13 (wrapper flow and apex `rp.id` WebAuthn).
- **T72.c** - items 11 and 12 (proxies, WebSocket/SSE, Cloudflare wildcard CNAME).

## Not in scope

Building the real content listener, prelude or wrapper (T16, T62, T29). The full §7 security suite (T38). The Telegram passkey *sign-in* real-device test (T38 records it; T72 item 13 checks the assertion mechanics only).

## Design notes and decisions

- Decision 10: no cookies on part hosts; every part is sandboxed; no separate registrable domain. The spike is the evidence for that decision on today's browsers.
- Known risk areas: WebKit blob workers, Safari ITP on credentialed same-site fetches, Telegram webview WebAuthn support. For each failure, the note must state the fallback the plan already names (e.g. item 1: list the host explicitly; the wrapper flow failing would reopen the hand-off design).
- Record browser versions exactly; re-run is cheap, so keep the rig in `tests/sandbox-spike/` until T38 replaces it.

## Acceptance criteria

dag2.py: "A results table in docs/spikes/sandbox.md, one row per §2 spike item per browser, each pass/fail with the browser version".

- [ ] `docs/spikes/sandbox.md` has a table: item x {Chrome, Safari macOS, iOS Safari, Firefox} (+ Telegram webview for item 13), each cell pass/fail + version + notes.
- [ ] Every fail has a stated consequence for T16/T29/T62 and the chosen fallback.
- [ ] The rig is checked in and re-runnable with one command.
- [ ] Completed before T16 work starts (record the date).

## Tests

The rig's pages self-report: each item page posts `{item, browser, ua, result, detail}` to a collector endpoint that appends JSONL to `target/sandbox-spike/results.jsonl`; a script renders the markdown table from it. Automated runs on Playwright Chromium/WebKit/Firefox (`tests/sandbox-spike/run.ts`), manual runs on iOS Safari and Telegram recorded with screenshots.

## References

PLAN.md §2 Serving a part (canonical CSP, caps in path, framed expiry, `/_rt`), §2 The link to post (wrapper flow), §3 Isolation of agent-written JS, §5 Maturity check (spike items 1-13), §6a T72 row, decision 10, §3a Admin (rp.id = apex).

## Dependency context (from the task graph)

- Phase: P0 (epic "P0 Core (local)"). Slice 1: yes. Priority P1.
- Task-graph acceptance test (verbatim): A results table in docs/spikes/sandbox.md, one row per §2 spike item per browser, each pass/fail with the browser version
- Depends on (must be closed first): 
  - T01: Cargo workspace, crate layout, CI (fmt, clippy, nextest, deny, audit), Renovate
- Unblocks: 
  - T16: Content listener (:8081): part-host routing, /c/<cap>/ serving, serve-time entry templating, canonical CSP
- Subtasks (children of this bead; close this bead only after all of them are closed and the acceptance criteria above pass end to end):
  - T72.a: Sandbox spike rig + items 1-9: opaque-origin semantics on Chrome, Safari, iOS Safari, Firefox
  - T72.b: Sandbox spike items 10 and 13: #pf= wrapper flow and apex rp.id WebAuthn (Safari, Telegram)
  - T72.c: Sandbox spike items 11 and 12: WebSocket/SSE from opaque origins through proxies; tunnel wildcard

## Logging (test convention)

Every test in this bead asserts the structured server events for the paths it exercises (`event=…` with `request_id` and `outcome`, per T18's field convention) and keeps the captured JSONL under `target/test-logs/<test>/` (unit/integration) or `target/e2e-logs/<spec>/server.jsonl` plus Playwright trace/HAR (browser specs) on failure, so a red run explains itself. Log lines never carry part content, tokens, caps or secrets (T18 redaction; T112 canaries). Spikes and UI-only subtasks log each item's result the same way.

## Goal and why

Agents make nicer, consistent pages when offered a design system (shartifacts' `get_design_system` idea). **Jig** is optional and deliberately distinct from the workspace chrome (Stockroom): parts should look like *their* content, not like partifactory, and must never imitate the chrome. Jig also styles the kinds partifactory renders itself (Markdown, text, slides, Mermaid, code), replacing the Slice 1 `pf-shell.css`. Jig is itself a part in the `system` space, so the admin edits it like anything else. After Slice 1 (P2), feeds M2.

## Scope

- **Delivery**: `jig.css` (≈ 8 KB brotli) as a shared blob at `https://blobs-part.<domain>/b/<hash>/jig.css` (allowed by the part CSP); opt in with one `<link>` and `data-jig` on `<html>`. The TSX import map exposes `@partifactory/jig` (CSS import + a tiny `useJigTheme()` hook) once T60 exists.
- **Tokens** (`--jig-*`), five root tokens you may override; everything else derives with `color-mix()` and `light-dark()`:
```css
:root[data-jig] {
  color-scheme: light dark;
  --jig-accent:  light-dark(#2f54c9, #8eaaf5);
  --jig-paper:   light-dark(#ffffff, #161719);
  --jig-ink:     light-dark(#1c1d1f, #e9e9e6);
  --jig-font:    system-ui, -apple-system, "Segoe UI", Roboto, sans-serif;
  --jig-radius:  8px;
  --jig-ink-2:     color-mix(in oklab, var(--jig-ink) 80%, var(--jig-paper));
  --jig-ink-3:     light-dark(#5f636a, #a3a6ab);
  --jig-surface:   color-mix(in oklab, var(--jig-ink) 4%, var(--jig-paper));
  --jig-surface-2: color-mix(in oklab, var(--jig-ink) 8%, var(--jig-paper));
  --jig-line:      color-mix(in oklab, var(--jig-ink) 14%, var(--jig-paper));
  --jig-line-strong: color-mix(in oklab, var(--jig-ink) 45%, var(--jig-paper));
  --jig-on-accent: light-dark(#ffffff, #0c1530);
  --jig-accent-wash: color-mix(in oklab, var(--jig-accent) 12%, var(--jig-paper));
  --jig-good: light-dark(#1d7a4a, #6fd0a1);
  --jig-warn: light-dark(#8a5200, #f2c25e);
  --jig-bad:  light-dark(#b42318, #ff8b80);
  --jig-mono: ui-monospace, "SF Mono", Menlo, Consolas, monospace;
  --jig-font-display: var(--jig-font);
  --jig-c1: #2f54c9; --jig-c2: #d97a1e; --jig-c3: #1d8a6b; --jig-c4: #c43d6b;
  --jig-c5: #7a5bc7; --jig-c6: #8a7a2a; --jig-c7: #2a8ab8; --jig-c8: #6b6f76;
  --jig-s1: 4px; --jig-s2: 8px; --jig-s3: 12px; --jig-s4: 16px; --jig-s5: 24px; --jig-s6: 40px; --jig-s7: 64px;
  --jig-t-xs: .8125rem; --jig-t-sm: .875rem; --jig-t-md: 1rem; --jig-t-lg: 1.25rem; --jig-t-xl: 1.75rem; --jig-t-2xl: 2.5rem;
}
```
  `data-jig-theme="light|dark"` pins the scheme.
- **Presets**: `data-jig-preset="paper"` (default), `"ledger"` (warm off-white, `ui-serif` display, green accent), `"slate"` (dark-first, cyan accent), `"kid"` (bigger type, rounder, high-saturation accent).
- **Classes** (plain CSS, no JS): Layout `.jig-page` (max 960), `.jig-wide` (1280), `.jig-stack`, `.jig-cluster`, `.jig-grid`, `.jig-split`, `.jig-sticky-bar`; Text `.jig-prose`, `.jig-eyebrow`, `.jig-lede`, `.jig-muted`, `.jig-num`; Surfaces `.jig-card`, `.jig-card--flat`, `.jig-callout` (`--good/--warn/--bad`), `.jig-divider`; Controls `.jig-btn` (`--primary/--quiet/--danger`), `.jig-input`, `.jig-select`, `.jig-check`, `.jig-switch`, `.jig-field`, `.jig-seg`, `.jig-tabs`; Data `.jig-table`, `.jig-stat`, `.jig-meter`, `.jig-progress`, `.jig-badge`, `.jig-tag`, `.jig-kbd`; States `.jig-empty`, `.jig-skeleton`, `.jig-toast`, `.jig-pending`; Docs/decks `.jig-slide`, `.jig-deck` (16:9, keyboard nav), `.jig-code`.
- **Rules shipped with it**: use the tokens, not hex; one accent per part; tables for data; `.jig-stat` for headline numbers; respect `prefers-color-scheme`; `base: './'` and relative URLs; `window.storage` for state; never imitate partifactory's chrome (no tag chips, part numbers or corner ticks).
- **Op `design_system`** (registry): read-only, returns `{rules, starter: {path: text}, jig_version, css_url}` read from the Jig part's live version (so editing the Jig part changes the output). Its section is added to `docs/agent-contract.md` in the same change (T56 test).
- **Jig is a part** in the `system` space (created by `provision`, T32); on boot, if absent, seed it from the embedded source. Publishing a new live version of Jig re-registers `jig.css` as a shared blob (`shared_blobs.source = 'seed'`); parts record `meta.jig_version`.
- Swap `pf-shell.css` for `jig.css` in the `markdown` and `text` renderers (T12 hook), with typography from Jig.

## Not in scope

Slides runtime and Mermaid/KaTeX (T66); Stockroom tokens (T31); TSX import map plumbing (T60).

## Design notes and decisions

- *Why distinct from chrome:* a part must never be mistaken for the workspace (phishing inside the frame).
- *Why a part:* the admin edits it with the same tools; versions and rollback come for free.
- Contrast: listed pairs (white on `#2f54c9` 6.5:1; `#0c1530` on `#8eaaf5` 7.9:1; ink 16.9/14.8; ink-3 6.0/7.4) are asserted.

## Acceptance criteria

dag2.py: "The op returns rules + starter; editing the Jig part changes the op output; Jig contrast test passes".
- [ ] `design_system` returns rules, a starter `index.html` using `data-jig`, and the CSS URL.
- [ ] A new Jig version changes `jig_version` and the CSS hash in the op output; old parts keep loading their recorded hash.
- [ ] Markdown and text kinds render with Jig after this task (golden render corpus updated).

## Tests

`crates/pf-ui/tests/jig_contrast.rs` (parses `jig.css`, computes WCAG pairs in light and dark, fails < 4.5:1 text); `crates/pf-ops/tests/design_system.rs::{returns_rules_and_starter, follows_jig_part_live}`; golden `tests/golden/render/markdown_jig/*`; Playwright screenshot of the starter in each preset.

## References

PLAN.md §4a.8 Part content, and the optional Jig design system; §2 Kinds (markdown/text shell); §3a Spaces (`system`); decision 19.

## Dependency context (from the task graph)

- Phase: P2 (epic "P2 Agents + the household loop"). Slice 1: no (after Slice 1). Priority P2.
- Task-graph acceptance test (verbatim): The op returns rules + starter; editing the Jig part changes the op output; Jig contrast test passes
